<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="5e00bb3a">&lt;p&gt;
    A stakeholder register is not the same as an organizational chart. Instead, it identifies the organizational and
    interpersonal relationships of people that will affect an engagement.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The success of a change initiative is often influenced by the unofficial or unrecognized relationships that exist
    between principal players within an organization, or from influences outside an organization such as unions.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Much of the value of this work product comes from thinking of the project from each stakeholder&apos;s perspective and
    incorporating that understanding into the management of the project.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    This work product is a useful onboarding reference for other project members.  Keep it updated over the life of
    the project.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="6e6b125e"></attribute>
<attribute name="usageGuidance" nodeId="f58a25ff"></attribute>
<attribute name="refinedDescription" nodeId="7e353cb">&lt;p&gt;
    This is a simple list of the people of the enterprise, both internal and external, which are affected by the project.
    The register identifies what role each stakeholder plays in the client organization, project team or suppliers to the
    project. The register is dynamic in that existing stakeholders may change in terms of their involvement or influence on
    the project.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The stakeholder register may include not only people directly involved with the delivery of the project or those that
    realize the benefits of the project outputs, but also any vendors, suppliers, customers, or shareholders who may be
    impacted by project outcomes.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
</Element>
